    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "51805a91_e578954f",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/connectivity/NetworkMonitor.java",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 1168,
      "author": {
        "id": 1107893
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-08-23T00:52:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Using FakeSettingsProvider is something I have considered, but to be honest I am not sure which way to go between this two approaches:\n\n1) for every class, create a custom dependency interface just for that class, such as NetworkMonitorSettings here, and mock it with Mockito + when().thenReturn() on a case by case basis.\n\n2) for all classes, create generic and reusable mocking interfaces such as FakeSettingsProvider.\n\n\nI see there can be value in 2) but I am a bit wary of going that road because I can foresee: a) lots of boilerplate to tie the generic mocking interfaces together in tests, b) maintenance issue as the scope of genericity and the number of users grows.\n\nI would like to start by doing 1) because I think it is more flexible. If it turns out there are obvious opportunities for common dependency interfaces, then I ll totally want to refactor them into the same thing as they arise.",
      "parentUuid": "9de2f65c_6e1e9f90",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1168,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 1168,
        "endChar": 18
